{"id":"6cfd1eb9-97cd-42ac-bdce-4a8aff754610","object":"chat.completion","created":1785033023,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    \\n    # Build maps\\n    node_map = {}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = node\\n        children_map.setdefault(node['parent'], []).append(nid)\\n    \\n    # Find root (parent None)\\n    root_id = None\\n    for nid, node in node_map.items():\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            root_id = nid\\n            break\\n    if root_id is None:\\n        raise ValueError('No root node')\\n    \\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then ID ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    \\n    retained_set = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    \\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained_set:\\n            continue\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        \\n        # Check for retained children\\n        has_retained_child = False\\n        for child_id in children_map.get(nid, []):\\n            if child_id in retained_set:\\n                has_retained_child = True\\n                break\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        \\n        # Collect all nodes in the subtree rooted at nid that are still retained\\n        subtree_ids = set()\\n        stack = [nid]\\n        while stack:\\n            current = stack.pop()\\n            if current in retained_set:\\n                subtree_ids.add(current)\\n                for child in children_map.get(current, []):\\n                    stack.append(child)\\n        \\n        # Check if any required symbol would be lost entirely\\n        # i.e., for each required symbol, if all occurrences in retained set are inside subtree_ids, cannot remove\\n        can_remove = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            # Find all retained nodes with this symbol\\n            all_with_sym = [n for nid2, n in node_map.items() if nid2 in retained_set and n['symbol'] == sym]\\n            # Count how many are outside subtree\\n            outside_count = sum(1 for n in all_with_sym if n['id'] not in subtree_ids)\\n            if outside_count == 0:\\n                can_remove = False\\n                break\\n        if not can_remove:\\n            continue\\n        \\n        # Remove the whole subtree\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        retained_set -= subtree_ids\\n    \\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained_set)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01 based on the problem description and the provided payload schema. The case contains a list of nodes (with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols. The task is about \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" – essentially a pruning algorithm that removes removable nodes while preserving required symbols and grammar validity. The description hints at a deterministic algorithm: deeper nodes before shallower, never remove root, required symbols, or nodes with retained children (i.e., if a node is removed, its children are also removed? Actually it says \"never remove a node with retained children\" meaning if you decide to keep a child, you cannot remove the parent? Or maybe it's about a tree where removal means deleting that node and all its descendants? Typically in tree reduction, removing a node would also remove its children because they become orphaned unless they are reattached. But here the description says \"tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Also \"never remove a node with retained children\" – that suggests if you keep any child, you cannot remove the parent. Because the children would lose their parent. So the algorithm likely works from leaves upward: you can remove a node if it's removable, not required, and all required symbols are still present in the tree after removal (including its children? If you remove a node, its children are also removed, so required symbols in its subtree would be lost unless they appear elsewhere). So we need to decide which nodes to remove.\n\nThe output requires two arrays: removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. So we need to produce the list of node IDs that are removed (in removal order) and those that are retained. The description mentions \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably alphabetically or by ID string). Removed_node_ids should be in the order of removal (the algorithm order). The algorithm: deeper nodes before shallower, using node ID as tie-breaker at same depth.\n\nSteps to implement:\n1. Build a tree from nodes list. Each node has id, parent (null for root), depth, removable, utility, symbol.\n2. Identify root (parent null).\n3. Determine required_symbols set.\n4. Build a set of all node IDs.\n5. We need to decide which nodes to remove. The algorithm is likely: iterate over nodes sorted by depth descending, then by ID (string sort) for tie-breaking. For each node, consider tentative removal: check if it is removable, not root, not required (i.e., its symbol not in required_symbols? Actually required_symbols are symbols that must be present in the tree after reduction. So a node with a symbol that is required cannot be removed if it's the only node with that symbol? But the description says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So \"a required symbol\" means a node that has a symbol that is in the required_symbols list? Or does it mean a node that is marked as required? The payload shows \"removable\" boolean, but no separate \"required\" field. The required_symbols list at top level indicates which symbols must remain. So nodes whose symbol is in required_symbols should not be removed? But careful: there might be multiple nodes with the same symbol. The requirement is that the set of symbols present in the retained tree must include all required symbols. So we can't remove a node if that would cause a required symbol to be absent. So we need to check symbol presence globally. So the rule \"never remove a required symbol\" is interpreted as: if a node's symbol is in required_symbols and that node is the only occurrence of that symbol in the current tree, then we cannot remove it. But the description says \"never remove ... a required symbol\" – maybe it means a node that is itself marked as required? But the payload doesn't have that. I think it's about the symbol: if a node's symbol is required, that node is \"a required symbol\". However, we can remove it if there's another node with the same symbol that remains. So we need to count occurrences.\n\nAlso \"never remove a node with retained children\" – if a node's child is going to be retained, then the parent cannot be removed. This implies that we process children before parents. So the traversal order (deeper first) ensures that when we consider a node, its children have already been decided (either removed or retained). If any child is retained, then this parent cannot be removed. If all children are removed, we can consider removing the parent, provided other conditions hold.\n\nThe description also mentions \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So after tentative removal, we check if the set of symbols of retained nodes (including this node's ancestors? Actually if we remove a node, its descendants are also removed. So we need to simulate removal of that node and its subtree. But we are processing bottom-up, so when we consider a node, its subtree has already been reduced (some children removed). So the current subtree's retained nodes are those that are not yet removed. So we need to know which nodes are currently considered retained (initially all). As we decide to remove nodes, we mark them as removed. So the algorithm:\n\n- Start with set of all node IDs as retained.\n- For each node in order (depth descending, then ID ascending):\n   - Check: if node is removable, and not root, and its symbol not required? Actually we need to check if after removing this node and all its (retained?) descendants (which will become removed because they have no parent?), the required symbols are still covered.\n   - But if we remove a node, all nodes in its subtree are also removed (since they depend on it). So we need to see if the symbols of the subtree (including the node itself) are also present elsewhere in the retained tree.\n   - So we compute the set of symbols that would be lost if we remove this node and its subtree (but the subtree may have already been partially removed; we only consider currently retained nodes in its subtree). Actually if we remove the node, all nodes in its subtree that are still retained will also be removed because they lose parent. So we need to check if the required symbols that appear only in this subtree would be lost.\n   - Condition: for every required symbol, there must be at least one retained node with that symbol outside the subtree of this node (or inside if we keep it). So if there is some required symbol that is only present in this node's subtree (after considering already removed nodes), then we cannot remove it.\n   - Also \"never remove a node with retained children\" is automatically enforced if we process bottom-up: if any child is retained (not yet removed), then we cannot remove this parent. So we check: for each child node, if it is still retained, then we must not remove parent.\n   - Additionally, the root should never be removed (parent null).\n   - The node's own removable flag must be true.\n\n- If conditions satisfied, we remove the node and all its subtree (i.e., mark them as removed). Record the node ID (and perhaps its children? but the output expects only removed_node_ids list, which is the IDs of removed nodes, presumably only the ones we explicitly remove, not all descendants? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" The retained IDs are all nodes not removed. The removed_node_ids should be the IDs of nodes that were removed in the order they were removed. But if we remove a parent, its children are also removed implicitly. Should they be included in removed_node_ids? The algorithm probably only records the direct removal action (the node we decide to remove). The children are also removed but they are not explicitly decided separately; they are removed because their parent was removed. However, the output may expect all removed node IDs (including indirectly removed ones). The phrase \"actual removal order\" suggests the order in which nodes were decided to be removed. In bottom-up traversal, we might remove leaf nodes individually when they are removable. For a parent removal, we would remove it, and its children might have already been considered but not removed (because they were retained or not removable). If we later remove the parent, then those children become removed. Should we add those children to removed_node_ids? The description says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It talks about removing a node, not its children. The children are removed implicitly. But the output should list all nodes that end up removed? In typical tree reduction, you'd list the nodes that are explicitly removed. However, the fixture may be deterministic. We need to infer the expected behavior from the case.\n\nLet's analyze the first fixture case (fg1-se-pe-01-b-000):\nNodes:\n0: root, parent null, symbol root, removable false, utility 1\n1: n1, parent root, symbol s1, removable false, depth1\n2: n2, parent root, symbol s2, removable true, depth1\n3: n3, parent root, symbol s0, removable false, depth1\n4: n4, parent n2, symbol s1, removable true, depth2\n5: n5, parent n3, symbol s2, removable false, depth2\n6: n6, parent n1, symbol s0, removable true, depth2\n\nRequired symbols: [root, s1]\n\nDepth order (descending): depth2 first: n4, n5, n6 (IDs: n4, n5, n6). Then depth1: n1, n2, n3. Then depth0: root.\n\nProcess depth2:\n- n4: removable true, parent n2 (still retained). Check retained children: none (no children). Check required symbols: after removing n4, symbols lost = s1. Is s1 present elsewhere? Yes, n1 has s1 and is retained. So condition passes. Also not root, not required (s1 is required but another node has it). So we can remove n4. Remove n4. Mark removed. removed_node_ids = [n4].\n- n5: removable false (already false), so cannot remove.\n- n6: removable true, parent n1 (retained). No children. Symbol s0, not required. Check required symbols: removing n6 loses s0, not required, so okay. So remove n6. removed_node_ids = [n4, n6].\n\nNow depth1:\n- n1: removable false, cannot remove.\n- n2: removable true, parent root. Check children: n4 was removed (so no retained children). Symbol s2, not required. Check required: after removing n2, we lose symbol s2 (but not required). But also, what about n4? Already removed. So no issue. However, we must check if any required symbol is only in n2's subtree. The only node in n2's subtree originally was n4, which had s1. But n4 is already removed. So removing n2 would not affect required symbols because s1 is still present in n1. So it seems we can remove n2. But note: n4 was removed but we already removed it, that's fine. So remove n2. removed_node_ids = [n4, n6, n2].\n- n3: removable false, cannot remove.\n\nDepth0: root removable false.\n\nNow retained nodes: root, n1, n3, n5? n5 was not removed, it's retained. So retained_node_ids = [root, n1, n3, n5] sorted alphabetically: ['n1', 'n3', 'n5', 'root']? Wait, root is 'root', so sorted string order: 'n1', 'n3', 'n5', 'root'. That seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to see if the expected answer is consistent with this reasoning. We don't have the expected output, but we can trust the algorithm.\n\nHowever, we need to consider \"grammar_valid\" - the tree must remain a valid tree after removals. Removing a node should also remove its descendants, which is natural.\n\nAlso note \"registered_candidate_order\" and \"stable_ties\" - we are using depth descending and ID ascending which is stable.\n\nThe algorithm above seems correct. But we must also handle the case where a node's children are all removed, then it becomes a leaf, and we might remove it. That is what we did.\n\nOne nuance: \"never remove a node with retained children.\" In our algorithm, we check children: if any child is still retained (i.e., not removed), then we cannot remove the parent. That's correct.\n\nAlso, we should consider that when we remove a node, we should also remove all its descendants from the retained set (even if we didn't explicitly remove them). But in our processing order (bottom-up), if a node's children were removed, we already accounted for that. If a node has retained children, we cannot remove it. So that's consistent.\n\nNow, what about nodes that are not removable? They stay.\n\nWhat about the root? Not removable.\n\nNow, we need to implement this in Python. The solve(case) function receives the payload object (with 'nodes' and 'required_symbols' and possibly '_case_nonce' but we ignore nonce). We'll parse it, build a dict mapping id to node data, also build tree children mapping.\n\nSteps in code:\n- Validate input structure: ensure 'nodes' list, each has required fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility (maybe not needed). We'll check.\n- Build parent_to_children dict.\n- Build node_map.\n- Find root (node with parent None or null).\n- Build required_symbols set.\n- Initialize retained_set = set(all node IDs).\n- Define removal order list.\n- Get sorted nodes by depth descending, then ID ascending. ID as string.\n- Iterate:\n   - If node_id not in retained_set: skip? Actually if it was already removed (e.g., if it was a child of a previously removed parent), it would not be in retained_set, so skip.\n   - Retrieve node.\n   - Check conditions:\n        - node['removable'] must be True.\n        - node_id != root_id.\n        - node['symbol'] is in required_symbols? If yes, we need to check if there is another retained node with same symbol outside this node's subtree. But the condition \"never remove a required symbol\" likely means the node itself is required? Since the only required symbols are in the list, and a node with that symbol might be the only one, so we need to check count. We'll implement: if node['symbol'] in required_symbols and that symbol appears only in this node's subtree (i.e., no other retained node anywhere else has that symbol), then cannot remove.\n        - Check retained children: for each child in children_map.get(node_id, []), if child_id in retained_set, then cannot remove.\n        - If all conditions pass, then we can tentatively remove. But we need to also check that after removal, all required symbols still have at least one retained node. Instead of checking only the node's own symbol, we need to check for all required symbols that might be lost due to removing this subtree. The subtree includes the node and all its retained descendants (since they will be removed). So we need to compute the set of symbols present in the subtree (including node) that are also required, and ensure that each such symbol appears elsewhere in the retained tree (outside the subtree). Alternatively, we can do: for each required symbol, check if there is at least one retained node with that symbol not in the subtree. If not, cannot remove. That's a more comprehensive check.\n   - If allowed, remove the node and all its descendants from retained_set. Add node_id to removal_order list. Note: we only add the node_id to removal_order, not all descendants. The output expects \"removed_node_ids\" in the order of removal. The indirect removals (children) might be considered as removed at the same time? But the specification says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" It might expect the list of IDs that were explicitly removed (i.e., the nodes we decided to remove). The children are not explicitly decided; they go away. The phrase \"actual removal order\" suggests the sequence of removal decisions. So we list only the nodes we chose to remove.\n- After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted list of IDs in retained_set.\n- removed_node_ids = removal_order (the order we removed them).\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nEdge cases: \n- If a node has children that were already removed, it's fine.\n- The root can't be removed.\n- No randomness.\n\nPotential issues: The algorithm may remove a parent after its children have been removed, which is fine.\n\nNow, we need to produce Python source code as a JSON string.\n\nWe need to output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being the Python source string. The source must define solve(case) and return a JSON-serializable dict.\n\nMake sure to handle imports? Only standard library. Use json? Not needed for return, we just return dict.\n\nWe'll write code in a string, escape newlines and quotes.\n\nLet's write the implementation."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":4343,"total_tokens":6515,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3671},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20